X127 — WHERE A WARM `tagstackEncode` ACTUALLY SPENDS ITS TIME, AND TWO FALSIFIED SPEED HYPOTHESES
=================================================================================================
Branch arena/6adc24e4-kompkernel, from 77e5e02. Node 22.22.3, o200k_base. All runs OMEGA_DET=1.

(g) FRONTIER RE-VERIFICATION (requirement: confirm before merging anything)
    Driver: bench/x125/x125-ab.ts bundled to bench/x126/x125ab29.mjs, ROUNDS=1.
    Result:  Σ OFF 12023  →  Σ ON 12020   Δ -3   (1 better / 0 worse / 28 same)
             every row decoded === src
             ps-aux-real  991 → 988
    Receipt: bench/x126/receipts/x127-frontier-reverify.log
    The Σ wall figure in that log (OFF 536.2s vs ON 295.8s, "-44.8%") is NOT a speed result.
    Whichever arm runs first inside a lane is 3-5x slower, in both directions:
        lsblk     OFF first 2.2s / ON 0.2s      kb-article ON first 3.0s / OFF 1.0s
        ps-aux    OFF first 52.7s / ON 11.7s    apt-real   ON first 80.4s / OFF 20.6s
    The two arms alternate as first across lanes, so the sums are not order-balanced. Do not cite it.

1. PROFILE (bench/x126/x127-profile.ts, warm encode of apt-real, 18.4s, --cpu-prof)
      16.4%  countLongRaw              (bpe.ts: the pretoken fold loop)
       8.2%  (anon)                    (cache probe closures)
       7.9%  rankAt                    (bpe-fast: byte-pair rank lookup)
       7.0%  scoreRunCostDeltaSlots    (ariadne: the probe scorer)
       6.1%  mergeCount                (bpe-fast: the BPE merge loop)
       6.1%  RegExp: PRETOK            (the o200k pretokenizer itself)
       6.0%  countTokens3 / 6.0% chunkCountFast / 3.5% fastChunkCount / 2.0% fastCount
       3.8%  garbage collector
       2.2%  encode @ encoding (TextEncoder), 1.9% renderBest, 1.8% prolepsisLookup, 1.2% envOff
   => ~55% of a warm lane is token counting. Counting is the only place a whole-stack win can live.

2. THE COUNT IS ALMOST ENTIRELY CACHE LOOKUPS, NOT BPE WORK (bench/x126/x127-cache-stats.ts)
   apt-real, warm:  chunk hits 38,613,291   misses 284,396   fallbacks 0   hit rate 99.3%
   cache size at end 284,396. i.e. the working set of chunks is small and 99.3% of the fold
   is served from memory; only 284k genuine BPE counts are performed per lane.

3. HYPOTHESIS A — "the count memo thrashes; raise the budget"    FALSIFIED (already, at X88)
   LONG_CACHE_MAX_TOTAL_CHARS defaults to 2,000,000 retained chars and evicts with clear().
   It is env-overridable, so it was tested directly with no code change:
       OMEGA_COUNT_MEMO_TOTAL_CHARS=2000000   17575ms / 18398ms
                                 =64000000   16993ms / 17026ms
                                 =256000000  16807ms / 16247ms
   ~-7% at 128x the memory, inside run-to-run noise, and X88 measured it the other way
   (478ms at 2M against 528ms at 32M). The long strings are mostly DISTINCT, so no budget helps.
   Closed lever. Do not re-ship.

4. HYPOTHESIS B — "SHORT_CACHE evicts with clear() and thrashes; use FIFO partial eviction"
   FALSIFIED. countTokens answers strings <24 code units from SHORT_CACHE (2,000,000 entries),
   and on overflow it discarded the WHOLE map. Replaced with FIFO deletion of the oldest
   quarter (a Map iterates in insertion order), default raised to 8,000,000, and an
   OMEGA_SHORT_CACHE_EVICT=clear toggle read per eviction so both arms can alternate inside
   one process. In-process alternating A/B, arms warmed in both orders, R=3, medians:
       apt-real  M identical 816 = 816   med clear=20222ms  fifo=20080ms  (-0.7%)
                                        min clear=19060ms  fifo=19153ms
   No effect: at 2M entries the map never overflows on this lane, so eviction policy is moot.

5. HYPOTHESIS C — "mergeCount is O(n^2) with two array shifts per merge; use a linked list"
   FALSIFIED, AND IT IS SLOWER. mergeCount kept token boundaries packed in `starts`/`ranks` and
   shifted BOTH arrays left by one on every accepted merge (~3n^2 element moves per count).
   Replaced with a doubly-linked list over boundary nodes (node index == byte offset, so
   `starts` is never written), the minimum scan walking the list from node 0 so a strict `<`
   still selects the leftmost minimum; OMEGA_MERGECOUNT_SHIFT=1 restores the old body.
   CORRECTNESS (bench/x126/x127-mergecount-diff.ts): 76,000 strings — 60,000 random over the
   real wire alphabets (greek, cyrillic, cjk, latin, digits, punctuation, ¶§±×÷→←∞≈≡) plus
   16,000 slices of apt-real / code-ts / kb-article — each compared against `countTokensExact`
   (the reference encoder):  list(new) mismatches 0,  shift(old) mismatches 0. The rewrite is
   exact on this sample.
   SPEED, in-process alternating A/B, both arms warmed, R=3, medians, M identical 816 = 816:
       apt-real  med old=18783ms  new=19181ms   (+2.1% — SLOWER)
                 min old=18644ms  new=18979ms
   Why: the two shifts were contiguous typed-array moves that V8 does well; the replacement
   scan chases SN[i] pointers through an Int32Array and loses more than the shifts cost.

VERDICT. Three speed hypotheses tested this turn, three falsified; no speed win was shipped and
both code changes were reverted (git checkout of src/lib/omega/bpe.ts and bpe-fast.ts) so the
tree carries only the investigation. Counting is ~55% of the warm cost, the chunk hit rate is
99.3%, and the residual 0.7% of genuine counts is not reachable by any cache-policy or
data-structure change tried here. The remaining live lever is to make FEWER countTokens calls,
i.e. to prune probes in the ARIADNE search — and note that scoreRunCostDeltaSlots ALREADY passes
`bestRun` as the branch-and-bound limit, so the cheap pruning is done.

6. SMALL-OUTPUT LANES (bench/x126/x127-smalloutput-probe.ts) — axis closed, no headroom found.
   The 317 tokens shipped raw (lsblk 60, uptime-w 80, free-m 76) are raw because the WHOLE
   stack declines them, not because one codec was never tried: ΣΤΉΛΗ is already inside
   tagstack.ts (4 references), as are synkrisis, bifront, kanon, akolouthia2, apographe, metron,
   hemera, diastema, stichomythia. Measured directly: raw == M on all three.
   Separately, tagstackTransform refuses any text that does not start with '<', so no
   non-markup lane can ever take that path regardless of structure.
